<div class="page method" data-foot="${t}">
  <span class="eyebrow">basis &amp; method</span>
  <h2 class="sec">How these numbers are built</h2>
  <p>Every figure is <b>deterministic arithmetic on the firm's current data</b>, not a forecast. People are connected to projects; hours follow from those connections and each project's phase; cost follows from each person's burdened rate; firm numbers are sums rolled up, never averages of averages.</p>
  <div class="subhead">prizma's terms, in standard a/e vocabulary</div>
  <table class="maptbl">
    <thead><tr><th>in this brief</th><th>standard term</th><th>note</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>firm yield</td><td class="mono">net multiplier</td><td class="mv">net revenue / direct labor</td></tr>
      <tr><td>break-even</td><td class="mono">break-even rate</td><td class="mv">overhead rate + 1.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>utilization</td><td class="mono">utilization rate</td><td class="mv">billable / total available hours</td></tr>
      <tr><td>burdened rate</td><td class="mono">DPE</td><td class="mv">salary + personnel expense</td></tr>
      <tr><td>non-project labor</td><td class="mono">indirect labor</td><td class="mv">a component of overhead</td></tr>
      <tr><td>stability margin · silo gap · fragility</td><td class="mono">prizma proprietary</td><td class="mv">no standard equivalent</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Where a value is not yet confirmed against a real proposal or timesheet, it carries an inherited assumption; the confidence indicator states how much of the result rests on confirmed data.</p>
</div>

